Luxury hospitality is not getting quieter. It is getting more specific — guests can tell
the difference between a room that was specified and one that was simply furnished, and
2026's most talked-about openings share a few things in common.

## Experience over excess

The properties drawing the longest waitlists this year are not the ones with the most
marble. They are the ones where every touchpoint — the weight of a door handle, the way a
headboard is upholstered, the temperature of a lobby's lighting at 6pm — was decided on
purpose. That takes a design and procurement process that talks to itself, not eight
separate suppliers working from eight separate briefs.

## Procurement gets a technology layer

Sourcing at scale across a dozen countries used to mean a spreadsheet and a great deal of
faith. AI-assisted procurement tools now flag lead-time risk, currency exposure and
finish-consistency issues before a purchase order is even raised — not replacing the
relationships that make sourcing work, but catching the problems those relationships used
to catch too late.

## Sustainability as a specification line, not a slogan

The properties getting this right are not putting a leaf logo on the menu. They are
specifying FSC-certified timber, low-VOC finishes and repairable — not disposable —
furniture, because owners increasingly ask for the certification paperwork before they ask
for the swatch book.

None of this is abstract for the teams building it. It shows up as a slightly longer
sourcing timeline, a slightly more detailed sample review, and a property that still looks
right in year eight, not just on opening night.
